WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.680
There was music from my neighbor’s house through the summer nights.
整个夏天，每到夜里，我邻居家都有音乐声传出。

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:11.670
In his blue gardens men and girls came and went like moths among the whisperings and the champagne and the stars.
在他那蓝色的花园里，男人和姑娘们像飞蛾一样来来往往，穿行于窃窃私语、香槟美酒和点点星光之间。

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:25.860
At high tide in the afternoon I watched his guests diving from the tower of his raft, or taking the sun on the hot sand of his beach while his two motor-boats slit the waters of the Sound, drawing aquaplanes over cataracts of foam.
下午涨潮时，我看着他的客人从他浮台的跳台上跳水，或是躺在他那片海滩灼热的沙子上晒太阳，而他的两艘汽艇则划破海峡的水面，拖着滑水板掠过瀑布般翻腾的白沫。

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:39.200
On week-ends his Rolls-Royce became an omnibus, bearing parties to and from the city between nine in the morning and long past midnight, while his station wagon scampered like a brisk yellow bug to meet all trains.
每逢周末，他的劳斯莱斯就成了公共汽车，从早上九点直到午夜过后很久，把一拨拨客人从城里接来，又送回城里；而他的旅行车则像一只轻快的黄色甲虫，一溜小跑地去接每一班火车。

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:50.600
And on Mondays eight servants, including an extra gardener, toiled all day with mops and scrubbing-brushes and hammers and garden-shears, repairing the ravages of the night before.
而每逢星期一，八个仆人，包括一个临时加雇的园丁，就拿着拖把、板刷、锤子和园艺剪辛苦劳作一整天，收拾前一夜留下的一片狼藉。

00:00:51.400 --> 00:01:02.250
Every Friday five crates of oranges and lemons arrived from a fruiterer in New York—every Monday these same oranges and lemons left his back door in a pyramid of pulpless halves.
每个星期五，都有五箱橙子和柠檬从纽约一家水果店送来；每个星期一，还是这些橙子和柠檬，却已成了一个个没有果肉的半边果皮，堆成金字塔，从他家后门运出去。

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:12.260
There was a machine in the kitchen which could extract the juice of two hundred oranges in half an hour if a little button was pressed two hundred times by a butler’s thumb.
厨房里有一台机器，只要男管家用大拇指把一个小按钮按上两百下，半小时内就能榨出两百个橙子的汁。

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:22.540
At least once a fortnight a corps of caterers came down with several hundred feet of canvas and enough colored lights to make a Christmas tree of Gatsby’s enormous garden.
一队承办宴席的人至少每两个星期来一趟，带着几百英尺长的帆布，还有许许多多的彩灯，足以把盖茨比偌大的花园装点成一棵圣诞树。

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:34.230
On buffet tables, garnished with glistening hors-d’oeuvre, spiced baked hams crowded against salads of harlequin designs and pastry pigs and turkeys bewitched to a dark gold.
自助餐桌上点缀着晶莹发亮的冷盘，香料烤火腿与花色斑斓的色拉、酥皮小猪和仿佛被施了魔法、变成深金色的火鸡挤在一起。

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:46.270
In the main hall a bar with a real brass rail was set up, and stocked with gins and liquors and with cordials so long forgotten that most of his female guests were too young to know one from another.
正厅里设了一个酒吧，配有真正的黄铜栏杆，备有各种杜松子酒和烈酒，还有一些被人遗忘已久的加香甜酒，久到他的女宾大多年纪太轻，分不清哪种是哪种。

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:58.980
By seven o’clock the orchestra has arrived, no thin five-piece affair, but a whole pitful of oboes and trombones and saxophones and viols and cornets and piccolos, and low and high drums.
到七点钟，乐队已经来了——不是什么单薄的五人小乐队，而是整整一乐池的双簧管、长号、萨克斯管、维奥尔琴、短号、短笛，还有低音鼓和高音鼓。

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:16.390
The last swimmers have come in from the beach now and are dressing up-stairs; the cars from New York are parked five deep in the drive, and already the halls and salons and verandas are gaudy with primary colors, and hair shorn in strange new ways, and shawls beyond the dreams of Castile.
最后一批游泳的人这时已经从海滩回来，正在楼上换衣服；从纽约来的汽车在车道上停了五排，各处厅堂、客厅和游廊里已是一片花哨艳丽：满眼的原色，剪成新奇古怪样式的头发，还有连卡斯蒂利亚人做梦也想不到的披肩。

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:33.540
The bar is in full swing, and floating rounds of cocktails permeate the garden outside, until the air is alive with chatter and laughter, and casual innuendo and introductions forgotten on the spot, and enthusiastic meetings between women who never knew each other’s names.
酒吧里正热闹非凡，一巡巡飘来荡去的鸡尾酒弥漫到外面的花园，直到空气里充满了闲谈和笑声，充满了随口而出的影射之词、当场就忘的介绍，还有女人们热情洋溢的相见——她们从来不知道彼此的姓名。

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:43.800
The lights grow brighter as the earth lurches away from the sun, and now the orchestra is playing yellow cocktail music, and the opera of voices pitches a key higher.
随着大地踉跄着背离太阳，灯光越来越亮，此刻乐队正演奏着金黄色调的鸡尾酒会音乐，人声交织的歌剧也升高了一个调。

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:50.190
Laughter is easier minute by minute, spilled with prodigality, tipped out at a cheerful word.
笑声一分钟比一分钟来得容易，毫不吝惜地泼洒出来，只消一句欢快的话，便倾泻而出。

00:02:50.990 --> 00:03:14.080
The groups change more swiftly, swell with new arrivals, dissolve and form in the same breath; already there are wanderers, confident girls who weave here and there among the stouter and more stable, become for a sharp, joyous moment the centre of a group, and then, excited with triumph, glide on through the sea-change of faces and voices and color under the constantly changing light.
一群群人变换得更快了，新客一到就壮大起来，转眼间散而复聚；已经有人在四处游荡——那些自信的姑娘在较为壮实、稳当的人中间穿来穿去，有那么鲜明而欢快的一刻成了一群人的中心，接着又因得胜而兴奋不已，在不断变幻的灯光下，继续翩然滑过千变万化的面孔、声音和色彩。

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:26.890
Suddenly one of these gypsies, in trembling opal, seizes a cocktail out of the air, dumps it down for courage and, moving her hands like Frisco, dances out alone on the canvas platform.
突然，这些吉卜赛女郎中的一位，穿着一袭颤动的蛋白石色衣裙，凌空抓过一杯鸡尾酒，一口灌下去壮壮胆，然后像弗里斯科那样舞动着双手，独自跳着舞走上帆布舞台。

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:39.450
A momentary hush; the orchestra leader varies his rhythm obligingly for her, and there is a burst of chatter as the erroneous news goes around that she is Gilda Gray’s understudy from the Follies.
片刻的寂静；乐队指挥殷勤地为她变换了节奏；一个错误的消息四处传开，说她是《富丽秀》歌舞团里吉尔达·格雷的替补演员，人群中随之爆发出一阵七嘴八舌的议论。

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:41.570
The party has begun.
派对开始了。

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:48.520
I believe that on the first night I went to Gatsby’s house I was one of the few guests who had actually been invited.
我相信，第一次去盖茨比家的那天晚上，我是少数几个真正受到邀请的客人之一。

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.960
People were not invited—they went there.
人们并没有受到邀请——他们就那么去了。

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:58.520
They got into automobiles which bore them out to Long Island, and somehow they ended up at Gatsby’s door.
他们坐进汽车，汽车把他们载到长岛，不知怎么的，他们最后就到了盖茨比的家门口。

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:08.710
Once there they were introduced by somebody who knew Gatsby, and after that they conducted themselves according to the rules of behavior associated with amusement parks.
一到那里，就有某个认识盖茨比的人替他们作个介绍，此后他们的一举一动就按游乐园里的那一套行为规矩来。

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:17.830
Sometimes they came and went without having met Gatsby at all, came for the party with a simplicity of heart that was its own ticket of admission.
有时他们来了又走，压根儿没见到盖茨比；他们怀着一颗单纯的心奔着派对而来，这份单纯本身就是入场券。

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.160
I had been actually invited.
我倒是真正受到了邀请的。

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:34.980
A chauffeur in a uniform of robin’s-egg blue crossed my lawn early that Saturday morning with a surprisingly formal note from his employer: the honor would be entirely Gatsby’s, it said, if I would attend his “little party” that night.
那个星期六一大早，一个身穿知更鸟蛋青色制服的司机穿过我家的草坪，送来他雇主的一封短笺，正式得出人意料：上面说，如果我能光临他当晚的“小小派对”，那将完全是盖茨比的荣幸。

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:47.240
He had seen me several times, and had intended to call on me long before, but a peculiar combination of circumstances had prevented it—signed Jay Gatsby, in a majestic hand.
他见过我好几次，早就打算登门拜访，只是种种情况奇特地凑在一起，使他未能如愿——落款是杰伊·盖茨比，笔迹庄严堂皇。

00:04:48.040 --> 00:05:01.240
Dressed up in white flannels I went over to his lawn a little after seven, and wandered around rather ill at ease among swirls and eddies of people I didn’t know—though here and there was a face I had noticed on the commuting train.
我穿上一身白色法兰绒衣裤，打扮得整整齐齐，七点刚过就到他的草坪上去了，在一圈圈打着旋儿、流来涌去的陌生人群中四处走动，颇有些局促不安——不过，零零星星也有几张我在通勤火车上留意过的面孔。

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:13.710
I was immediately struck by the number of young Englishmen dotted about; all well dressed, all looking a little hungry, and all talking in low, earnest voices to solid and prosperous Americans.
散布在各处的年轻英国人之多，一下子就让我吃了一惊；他们个个衣着考究，个个都带着几分饿相，也个个都在低声恳切地同殷实富足的美国人交谈。

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:18.990
I was sure that they were selling something: bonds or insurance or automobiles.
我敢肯定，他们是在推销什么东西：债券、保险或者汽车。

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:27.280
They were at least agonizingly aware of the easy money in the vicinity and convinced that it was theirs for a few words in the right key.
他们至少都焦灼地意识到，近旁就有唾手可得的钱，并且深信只要用恰到好处的调子说上几句，这钱就归他们了。

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:48.870
As soon as I arrived I made an attempt to find my host, but the two or three people of whom I asked his whereabouts stared at me in such an amazed way, and denied so vehemently any knowledge of his movements, that I slunk off in the direction of the cocktail table—the only place in the garden where a single man could linger without looking purposeless and alone.
我一到就设法寻找主人，可我向两三个人打听他在哪里，他们都那样惊愕地瞪着我，又那样激烈地否认知道他的行踪，弄得我只好悄悄溜到鸡尾酒桌那边去——整个花园里，只有在那儿，一个独自前来的男子才可以逗留下去，而不显得无所事事、孤零零的。
